\section{Formulating a Hypothesis}
\begin{itemize}
  \item What is a hypothesis?
\end{itemize}

A hypothesis is an explanation that is testable

\begin{itemize}
  \item Why do we hypothesize?
\end{itemize}

To form a logical explanation for what you have observed.

\begin{itemize}
  \item How is a hypothesis different from an educated guess?
\end{itemize}

An educated guess is an assumption or conjecture; it is not a testable hypothesis. - What is the difference between a hypothesis and a theory?

A theory is a hypothesis that has been tested and supported many times and has become widely accepted.

\begin{itemize}
  \item How do you form a hypothesis?
\end{itemize}

Start with a question that can be answered with a "yes" or "no" answer.

\begin{itemize}
  \item What is the difference between an independent variable and a dependent variable?
\end{itemize}

A dependent variable is the one that you measure or observe in your experiment. The independent variable is the variable that you manipulate in the experiment to affect the dependent variable.

\begin{itemize}
  \item What do we mean by the experimental group and the control group?
\end{itemize}

The experimental group is the group that is exposed to the independent variable in the experiment. The control group is the group that is not exposed to the independent variable.

\section{Formulating a Hypothesis}
\section{Example:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item You notice that the local library's children's area is unusually quiet and the shelves are empty.

  \item You read about plans to renovate the children's area.

  \item You hypothesize that the reason for the empty shelves and quiet area in the children's area is that the library is renovating the area.

  \item Your hypothesis is testable, because you can observe what happens in the library after the renovation is completed.
